<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tharangambadi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tranquebar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tharangambadi, formerly Tranquebar, is a coastal town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-east-india-company">Danish East India Company</a> obtained it in 1620 from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-dansborg">Fort Dansborg</a>, making it the capital of Danish India until it was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1845. It was also the base of the first Protestant mission in India, founded in 1706.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tharangambadi.jpg" alt="Tharangambadi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esben Agersnap · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Dansborg.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
